In <2hkd2u$c47@milhouse.oec.com> tonyh@outland.oec.com (Tony Hsu) writes:
>I was wondering if DCE has a way to do "impersonation",
>for example, can I write a server that respond to client request
>and forward client credential to another server, and have the
>other server check the ACL, instead of embedding the ACL in
>this "gateway" server?

C --> S1 --> S2.

You want S2 to make is authentication checks based on C, not on S1.

You can do this in an application-specific way.  You write S1 and S2
such that S1 sends C's PAC as an explicit parameter in all the calls
S1 makes to S2.  When S2 gets a request, it sees if S1 is making the
request (which should be authenticated, of course) and if so it uses
the C-pac-parameter in its ACL check.  Otherwise it uses the credentials
obtained from the client binding handle.

DCE1.1 will have a general "delegation" mechanism, with native support
for delegation added to ACL's (there are a handful of new ACL entry types,
e.g.), and so on.
	/r$


