It has been my experience with DECW$SESSION that the "End Session"
option always kills *ALL* X-windows sessions.  This apparently was a
security decision by DEC. The "Quit" option will terminate DECW$SESSION,
and it's sub-processes, without killing other X-windows tasks.  However,
it does not appear on any of the "default" menus.  Use the 
"Options/Menus..." menu to add it to one of the default menus (I added
it to the "Session" menu).

However, this still leaves you with the problem of the Session manager
terminating all X-sessions when it "dies".  I got so tired of this that
I gave up using the session manager and used FileView instead.  It has a
"Quit" option on the "Session" menu which terminates FileView without
losing your other X-sessions and it doesn't shut your server down when
it dies.  Unfortunately you FileView doesn't seem to support
"auto-starting" applications (great big sigh here!).  I decided the
inconvenience of manully starting applications was less than the
inconvenience of losing everything.

> 
> After some of the responses I tried the variation of running DECW$SESSION
> instead.  This didn't have any effect on the speed of the mouse, but it still
> killed my X-windows server when I exited the session on the remote machine.
> 

What version of DecWindows are you running?  The "old" session manager
did all kinds of "setup" including setting your mouse acceleration. 
This shouldn't happen with the new session manager unless your are
exectuting DECW$STARTSM.COM which runs DECW$WSINIT.  Either way try
checking the mouse acceleration on the "pointer" menu of the session
manager.

